.TH CCOL_CHAN_TIMED_SEND_ZC 3 "2026-10-01" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
ccol_chan_timed_send_zc \- send a message with a timeout
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cthreadcomm.h>

ccol_retval_t ccol_chan_timed_send_zc(ccol_channel *ch,
                                  c_message_t *msg,
                                  uint64_t timeout_us);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
This function blocks and waits for free space in the queue that the channel
picks itself. It waits for the given timeout at most.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
ch          Channel to send through
msg         Message to send. On success the ownership of the data moves to
            the channel.
timeout_us  Longest time to wait, in microseconds, from the call. 0 does
            not wait: the call is then ccol_chan_try_send_zc.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
ccol_success              - The function sent the message
ccol_invalid_args         - ch or msg is NULL, or the check of msg
                            fails
ccol_not_permitted        - Sends on the chosen direction are turned off
ccol_container_full       - The timeout is 0 and the chosen queue is
                            full
ccol_timed_out            - The timeout ended
ccol_unexpected_failure   - The wait failed with an error other than a
                            timeout; errno holds that error
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
The function blocks for the duration of the timeout at most.
The function picks the direction itself from the ID of the thread.
A timeout of 0 does not wait. The call then does exactly what
ccol_chan_try_send_zc does, and returns its result: ccol_container_full,
and not ccol_timed_out, when it cannot proceed at once.
A timeout too large to form a deadline, such as UINT64_MAX, saturates
to the latest deadline that the clock can hold and in practice waits
with no end; it never wraps into the past.
The library measures the timeout against CLOCK_MONOTONIC. A change to the
wall clock of the system does not make the timeout longer or shorter. An
administrator, NTP, or the resume of a virtual machine can change that
clock. The call waits for the duration that the caller gave.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR ccol_chan_send_zc (3),
.BR ccol_chan_try_send_zc (3)
